  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 AM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nAnother night is upon us and, once again, it is more monsoonal \nactivity with some showers and isolated storms scattered through the \narea, although as I write this around 1 am most are west and south \nof Wyoming. These will continue through the night as well as this \nmorning. And the main story for the next seven days will be, once \nagain, the ebbs and flows of the monsoon, although we also have \nsomething else to talk about.\n\nFor the next 24 hours we have two concerns. The first concerns the \nconvection. Overall, things looks somewhat less active today. Most \nguidance does show drier air spreading across much of the area from \nwest to east starting this morning, but especially so this \nafternoon and evening as a cold front and trough move through the \narea. It could end up being below normal for the \nfirst time in a while across most of the area. There will still be \none area of concern though, and that is largely south of a Casper to \nEvanston line. Moisture will linger here and so will the chance of \nheavy rain. Precipitable water remains around 150 percent of normal \nhere. especially in southern Sweetwater County which has a slight \nrisk of heavy rainfall, with a marginal chance further north. The \nmain mitigating factor again is that there are decent low to mid \nlevel winds so the storms will be moving, so any threat of local \nflooding would be from training convection. Further north, the main \nthreat from storms would shift to strong wind gusts, as we have \nlarger dew point depressions, higher cloud bases, steep lapse rates \nas well as inverted V soundings. This should also be an earlier \nshow, as most guidance shows the dry air making all the way to the \nsouthern border, and this should bring a dry period from late \ntonight to Monday morning. And, with the threat of heavier rain \nending further north, we will likely cancel the flood watch sometime \nthis morning.\n\nThe other concern is the coolest temperatures so far this season. \nThere will be cooler air moving in behind the front, with lows \nfalling into the 30s for many. The question is the concern for a \nfrost or freeze. Temperatures have trended a bit warmer this \nmorning. The only lower elevation locations with a greater than 2 \nout of 5 chance of lows below 32 degrees is the northern Jackson \nvalley and around Bondurant. As for 28 degrees of below, the chance \nis less than 1 out of 4 everywhere. So, we will not issue a freeze \nwatch at this time. If we still issued frost advisories, I would \nhave considered one though. Just about all locations will have the \ncoolest night they have seen in a while.\n\nMonday should start dry. However, the monsoonal moisture will begin \npushing northward again, and once again increase the chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms, especially in southern and western \nWyoming. It may take a while to arrive though, with the deepest \narriving after sunset. This may limit heavy rain potential somewhat \nwith the lack of instability, despite precipitable water values once \nagain rising to over 150 percent at times, especially in southern \nWyoming. And with southwest flow returning, warmer temperatures will \nreturning to somewhat above normal levels. \n\nThe rest of the forecast period is again dependent on the movement \nof the monsoonal moisture. At this point, Tuesday looks to be more \nactive with above normal precipitable waters and a shortwave moving \nthrough the area. Wednesday or Thursday could then be less active \nas drier air moves in from the west with another uptick in \nconvection for Friday and into the weekend. However, the models \nhave been terrible in predicting these ups and downs in the \nmonsoonal moisture so may confidence in timing the surges of \nmoisture is low at best. With the southwest flow continuing, \ntemperatures will remain above normal. With the center of the \nridge possibly retrograding back to the west toward next \nweekend, it could get quite warm again with highs in the 90s \npossible for the upcoming holiday weekend. So, it continues to \nmore of the same with the monsoon continuing to dominate the \nweather conversation.",
